  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nWarmer than normal temperatures for early October and mostly dry \nconditions persist through much of the extended forecast period. On \nFriday, temperatures are expected to be a touch cooler than today \nand Thursday, thanks to decreasing thicknesses from the inverted \nupper trough. Highs are anticipated to be in the upper 70s to upper \n80s regionwide. Some CAMs and medium range guidance have isolated \nstorms Friday afternoon for portions of the Big Bend and far Lower \nTrans Pecos (Terrell County), though chances will be very low (~10-\n15%) given favorable surface moisture from Isaias is going to be \nfarther east. By the weekend, subtle upper-level ridging returns as \nthe aforementioned trough lifts northeast of the region. This sets \nup a dry and warm weekend where temperatures rebound back into the \nmid 80s to low 90s regionwide. By early next week, long-range \nguidance has an upper-level system approaching from the West Coast, \nwhile a tropical disturbance moves near the Gulf of California. \nUpper-level moisture from the tropical disturbance combined with \nsurface troughing across the Rockies will supply low (10-30%) rain \nchances late Monday into Tuesday for far west Texas and southeast \nNew Mexico. Cooler temperatures and increased rain chances are \nexpected by mid to late next week as both aforementioned weather \nsystems move closer to the region. There is quite a bit of \ndisagreement amongst model guidance in the strength, timing, and \npositioning of these systems. Details on potential weather impacts\nfor the forecast area should be ironed out as we get closer time.\nStay tuned!\n\nLamberson",
